WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.344 --> 00:00:02.677
[crunching]

00:00:05.944 --> 00:00:08.194
[pounding]

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:10.950
This is the classic first fight scene

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:12.869
in "Kill Bill: Vol. 1."

00:00:12.869 --> 00:00:15.119
[whacking]

00:00:16.983 --> 00:00:18.120
[glass breaking]

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.593
Now, look at this scene
again, but without sound.

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:26.780
Foley sounds tend to go unnoticed,

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:28.990
but you realize just
how important they are

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:30.310
when they're gone.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:33.780
Shaun Brennan, a Foley
artist for movies, TV,

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.850
and video games, showed
us what kinds of sounds

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.191
can really make a fight scene pop.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.543
Foley sounds are created
practically and based on

00:00:45.543 --> 00:00:48.600
a character's movements and
interactions with the world.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.640
Naturally, they're a big
part of fight scenes.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.720
Brennan has done Foley
sounds for a variety

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:54.860
of fight scenes.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:57.510
We met with him at Parabolic in New York.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.010
Brennan: It depends on
what's going on in the scene.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.270
If it's a real quiet moment,
I try to make it sound

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:02.700
as natural as possible.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.334
But if you're in a fight

00:01:04.334 --> 00:01:06.268
and there's a lot of noise going on,

00:01:06.268 --> 00:01:07.770
I will go over the top.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.030
Narrator: Foley isn't used
for every single sound

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:11.260
in a fight scene.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.270
Certain sounds need to
be created digitally,

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:17.198
but there are a few crucial
elements to every fight scene

00:01:17.198 --> 00:01:19.915
where the Foley artist's
work is necessary.

00:01:19.915 --> 00:01:21.217
Brennan: With the hand-to-hand stuff,

00:01:21.217 --> 00:01:23.160
sometimes I'll go home covered in bruises.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:24.910
Narrator: For the most part, the sounds of

00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:28.177
hand-to-hand combat are made
exactly as you would expect.

00:01:28.177 --> 00:01:29.946
Brennan: So, when doing punch sounds,

00:01:29.946 --> 00:01:32.738
I'll always do, like, a
real natural-sounding punch.

00:01:32.738 --> 00:01:35.630
[pounding]

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:36.589
Got that, or

00:01:36.589 --> 00:01:38.960
[slapping]

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:40.660
punch in the arm.

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.885
Or sometimes then after
that, I'll beef it up.

00:01:42.885 --> 00:01:45.030
[thumping]

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.270
Narrator: A pair of gloves
can add some extra thud

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:48.430
to a punch.

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:50.190
These sounds can work on their own

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.075
or be layered together,

00:01:52.075 --> 00:01:54.190
meaning just one single punch can contain

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:55.940
three separate sounds,

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:58.530
and, in some cases, even more than that.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:01.090
We'll get to layering a bit later.

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:03.320
Even a sound as simple as a punch

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:06.414
can involve some thinking outside the box.

00:02:06.414 --> 00:02:08.093
Brennan: In this case,
we have a flank steak.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:13.350
A piece of steak like this is just,

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:15.900
it's very similar to a
human cheek, you know.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.500
Narrator: This helps create
a fleshier punch effect,

00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.390
perfect for a face punch.

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:23.260
Plus, it doesn't just sound
like a character is simply

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:24.970
punching their own hand.

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:27.110
And, of course, you can't
have a fight without

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:29.051
a few solid kicks.

00:02:29.051 --> 00:02:31.790
[pounding]

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:34.280
And, who knows, maybe
you'll even have to kick

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:35.641
a door down.

00:02:35.641 --> 00:02:37.030
[kicking]

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:40.000
Just don't go into a fight empty-handed.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.413
Some of these sounds, like
bullets flying through the air,

00:02:43.413 --> 00:02:45.400
which involve no human interaction,

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:48.030
will be created separately
by a sound editor.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:50.830
But somebody still has to handle a gun.

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:52.630
Brennan often uses a door lock

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:54.459
and puts it against a rifle.

00:02:54.459 --> 00:02:56.876
[clattering]

00:03:05.818 --> 00:03:07.470
When it comes to weapons,

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:10.740
using the real thing is
typically the best option.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.287
Brennan: Well here's
some good-looking swords.

00:03:13.287 --> 00:03:14.313
[swooshing]

00:03:14.313 --> 00:03:15.271
[clanking]

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.300
But the problem is they're decorative, so.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:21.340
There's a lot of plastic on here;

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:22.398
they don't sound great.

00:03:22.398 --> 00:03:24.648
[clanking]

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:28.440
But any hits...sounds cheap.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.130
So, having something
heavier, some metal stuff,

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:33.635
sounds much better.

00:03:33.635 --> 00:03:35.160
[clanking]

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:36.471
Get these kind of sounds.

00:03:36.471 --> 00:03:38.721
[clanking]

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:01.260
Narrator: Usually, one
Foley artist will make

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:03.770
sound for both sides of a given fight.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:07.360
Using the real weapons creates
the most realistic sounds.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.460
To get that iconic sound of
swords whooshing in the air,

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:13.952
Foley artists will
typically use wooden dowels.

00:04:13.952 --> 00:04:16.285
[whooshing]

00:04:23.783 --> 00:04:25.700
[laughing]

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:26.930
Brennan: Not very realistic at all,

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:28.440
but it sounds great in the film.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.400
It's still a human element, just...

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:32.160
you've heard that before,

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.760
say, if a baseball flies
by you or something.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:36.310
Narrator: The dowels also work well for

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:38.220
kicking and flying through the air.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:41.200
But some of the most
complex, eye-popping sounds

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:43.270
don't have anything to do with weapons,

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:45.290
but rather the injuries.

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:47.350
When creating the Foley
sounds for injuries,

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:49.750
things can get a little messy.

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:52.897
Food especially comes in handy here.

00:04:52.897 --> 00:04:55.090
Brennan: Celery's really
good for bone cracks.

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:56.303
Say you break an arm.

00:04:57.624 --> 00:05:00.520
[cracking]

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:02.282
Give it a nice little
[makes crunching sound].

00:05:02.282 --> 00:05:03.841
Gives it more impact.

00:05:03.841 --> 00:05:04.991
[crunching]

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:14.000
Narrator: And if you wanna
get a good stomp in there,

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.103
then you'll have to step on a few apples.

00:05:17.357 --> 00:05:19.690
[crunching]

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:29.550
Meanwhile, other foods,
like raw chicken and melon,

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:32.440
can also give you good, fleshy sounds.

00:05:32.440 --> 00:05:35.650
Smashing a watermelon can
give you an exploding head.

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:38.310
And while not edible, a wet leather towel

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:40.282
sounds just as gruesome.

00:05:40.282 --> 00:05:42.532
[pounding]

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:48.150
A lot of these sounds could
end up being drowned out

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:49.420
in the final cut.

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:51.973
Yet, some of the most important
sounds Brennan must capture

00:05:51.973 --> 00:05:54.600
for a fight are the ones
you would least expect,

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:56.464
like the rustling of clothes.

00:05:56.464 --> 00:05:58.714
[rustling]

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:02.510
Brennan: And so, when
the actor goes to punch,

00:06:02.510 --> 00:06:05.076
I just follow the arm.

00:06:05.076 --> 00:06:08.130
[rustling]

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:09.850
Narrator: This is yet
another sound the audience

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:12.070
is familiar with, and
therefore one that can

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:14.070
help bring them into the scene.

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:16.110
These raw sounds are likely not the only

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:17.870
thing you'll hear in the final product.

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:19.670
There's an extensive process that follows

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:22.350
in which sounds are edited
and layered together.

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:24.680
Combining all the different
layers of the punch sound

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.750
we heard earlier really brings it to life.

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:29.452
Vinny Alfano: So, here is
just the regular punch.

00:06:29.452 --> 00:06:31.590
[pounding]

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:33.069
And then here's with the glove.

00:06:33.069 --> 00:06:35.540
[pounding]

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:37.740
And, you know, right now they're
not really mixed together,

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:40.620
but already you can start
to feel it being heavier

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:42.679
as far as the impact.

00:06:42.679 --> 00:06:44.929
[pounding]

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:52.766
It can get pretty gross.

00:06:52.766 --> 00:06:54.050
[laughing]

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:55.850
Narrator: So, sure, your
fight scene can have

00:06:55.850 --> 00:06:59.140
the coolest moves and feature
the fiercest warriors,

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:01.573
but it will be nothing
without the right sound.

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:05.753
Brennan: See ya, sucker!

00:07:06.705 --> 00:07:08.953
[laughing]

